She had a body that reached out and slapped my face like a five-pound ham-hock tossed from a speeding truck.


Realising that her symptoms indicated a virtually undetectable, fast acting neurotoxin, CIA coroner Quinn Abner frantically wrote up the details, lay on the floor and, as a professional courtesy, did her best to draw a chalk outline of herself. 


She was a beautiful woman; more specifically she was the kind of beautiful woman who had an hourlong skincare routine that made her look either ethereal or like a glazed donut, depending on how attracted to her you were.   

